# User changelog (Pyre)

[`nornir-pyre/pyre/CHANGELOG.user.md`](../../nornir-pyre/pyre/CHANGELOG.user.md) ships in the Windows
installer. Pyre shows it from **Help → What's New** and once after each upgrade. Write it for people
who use Pyre, not for developers. CI copies each version section into the GitHub Release body and
regenerates `docs/packages/pyre_changelog.rst` via `release/sync_pyre_user_changelog.py`.

- A commit that changes what a Pyre user can see or do (behaviour, a fix they could notice, a hotkey,
  menu, dialog, preference or default) adds a bullet to the newest section of `CHANGELOG.user.md` in
  the same commit.
- One `## <version> — <date, or "not yet released">` heading per version, **newest first**. Older
  versions stay below (append history; do not delete). The newest heading matches
  `nornir-pyre/pyproject.toml`: when that version changes, rename the unreleased heading. When a
  version ships, replace `not yet released` with the release date (`YYYY-MM-DD`).
- Write bullets for users: what changed and how to use it, naming menus, buttons and hotkeys as Pyre
  shows them. Leave out internal refactors and build changes. Notes only developers need go in an
  HTML comment (`<!-- -->`), which Pyre does not show.
- Start each bullet with a **bold summary of one short sentence or less** that a reader can skim
  (a fix keeps `Fix:` inside the bold), then the full detail as plain text that does not repeat the
  summary, for example
  `- **Fix: Spacebar freeze on warped grids.** Spacebar registration on damaged or heavily warped
  grids no longer freezes the UI…`.
- Supported Markdown: `#` / `##` headings, `-` or `*` bullets, paragraphs, `**bold**`, `` `code` ``
  and links. Keep prior version sections intact when adding a new one at the top.
